1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:04,784
Welcome to Project 1.

2
00:00:05,064 --> 00:00:10,084
In this course, you build a real analytics project with dbt and DuckDB.

3
00:00:10,364 --> 00:00:17,874
You start from an empty folder, and finish with a tested, automated pipeline, all inside Rosetta DBT Studio.

4
00:00:18,824 --> 00:00:26,912
We start with a full year of New York City green taxi trips: twelve monthly parquet files, and a small lookup table of taxi zones.

5
00:00:27,862 --> 00:00:31,687
dbt turns the raw files into one clean model of trips.

6
00:00:32,637 --> 00:00:40,430
On top of it, three small tables: trips per month and borough, the busiest pickup zones, and a monthly summary of how riders pay.

7
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:45,728
We add tests that catch bad data, and documentation with a lineage graph.

8
00:00:46,678 --> 00:00:55,023
Finally, GitHub Actions rebuilds everything on every change, and publishes the finished DuckDB database, so anyone can query it.

9
00:00:55,973 --> 00:01:00,424
The data comes from the New York City Taxi and Limousine Commission, the TLC.

10
00:01:00,704 --> 00:01:08,080
Every trip has its pickup and drop-off time, the pickup and drop-off zones, the distance, how the rider paid, and what it cost.

11
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:16,242
For 2025, that is about five hundred ninety thousand trips, in twelve files of about one megabyte each.

12
00:01:16,522 --> 00:01:18,898
Small enough to build on a laptop in seconds.

13
00:01:19,848 --> 00:01:23,416
A second file maps every zone number to its name and borough.

14
00:01:23,696 --> 00:01:30,011
Zone seventy-four, for example, is East Harlem North, in Manhattan, the busiest pickup zone of the year.

15
00:01:30,961 --> 00:01:33,643
You need one app: Rosetta DBT Studio.

16
00:01:33,923 --> 00:01:42,341
It is open source, it installs Python and dbt Core for you, and it gives you an editor, a terminal, lineage, and git, in one window.

17
00:01:43,291 --> 00:01:44,970
DuckDB is the database.

18
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:49,442
It lives in a single file on your computer, so there is no server to set up.

19
00:01:49,722 --> 00:01:53,229
And GitHub keeps your code, and later runs the pipeline for you.

20
00:01:54,179 --> 00:01:58,220
The whole project lives in one public repository on GitHub.

21
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:03,255
We create it in lesson 2.1, and every lesson ends with a commit and a push.

22
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:07,068
So each lesson's commit is the code for that lesson.

23
00:02:07,348 --> 00:02:10,719
The link to the repository is in every video description.

24
00:02:11,669 --> 00:02:13,485
Build your own copy as you watch.

25
00:02:13,765 --> 00:02:17,028
And if you get stuck, compare it with the course repository.

26
00:02:17,978 --> 00:02:19,541
The course has three modules.

27
00:02:19,821 --> 00:02:23,114
First, set up: install Rosetta DBT Studio.

28
00:02:24,064 --> 00:02:34,191
Second, dbt basics: create the project and connect it to GitHub, get the data, then models, Jinja, properties, docs, materializations, and tests.

29
00:02:35,141 --> 00:02:42,012
Third, ship it: automate the build with GitHub Actions, and turn the repository into a portfolio project.

30
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:47,926
You need a computer, a GitHub account and the basics of git, and some basic SQL.

31
00:02:48,206 --> 00:02:51,227
No dbt installed yet: we start from zero.

32
00:02:51,507 --> 00:02:52,253
New to git?

33
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:54,044
There are links in the description.

34
00:02:54,324 --> 00:02:55,749
See you in the first lesson.

35
00:03:03,683 --> 00:03:09,276
In this lesson, you install Rosetta DBT Studio, the one app we use for the whole course.

36
00:03:09,556 --> 00:03:15,933
You download it, copy it to your computer, and its first-run setup installs Python and dbt Core for you.

37
00:03:16,883 --> 00:03:20,867
Studio is open source, and every version is published on GitHub.

38
00:03:21,147 --> 00:03:25,142
Open the releases page of rosettadb, dbt-studio.

39
00:03:25,422 --> 00:03:28,118
The latest release is 1.7.6.

40
00:03:29,068 --> 00:03:30,431
Scroll down to Assets.

41
00:03:30,711 --> 00:03:39,600
There is a build for every system: a dmg for Mac, an exe for Windows, and deb, rpm and flatpak packages for Linux.

42
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:47,636
On a Mac, pick the dmg for your processor: arm64 for Apple silicon, x64 for Intel.

43
00:03:47,916 --> 00:03:51,775
This Mac has an Intel chip, so click the x64 dmg.

44
00:03:52,725 --> 00:03:54,965
When the download finishes, open the file.

45
00:03:55,245 --> 00:03:59,421
A window shows the app on the left, and your Applications folder on the right.

46
00:04:00,371 --> 00:04:05,528
Drag Rosetta DBT Studio onto Applications, and wait for the copy to finish.

47
00:04:06,478 --> 00:04:08,300
Then open it from Applications.

48
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:13,748
The first time, macOS asks whether you want to open an app downloaded from the internet.

49
00:04:14,028 --> 00:04:14,811
Click Open.

50
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:17,511
Studio starts with a short setup.

51
00:04:17,791 --> 00:04:19,642
First, the dbt version.

52
00:04:19,922 --> 00:04:22,997
Choose dbt 1.x, the recommended one.

53
00:04:23,947 --> 00:04:25,219
Then the exact version.

54
00:04:25,499 --> 00:04:28,098
Keep the latest, 1.12.6.

55
00:04:29,048 --> 00:04:33,236
Next, the adapters, one for each database dbt can talk to.

56
00:04:33,516 --> 00:04:39,641
This course only needs DuckDB, so uncheck the others, keep dbt-duckdb, and click Continue.

57
00:04:40,591 --> 00:04:42,924
Skip the optional tools, and click Install.

58
00:04:43,874 --> 00:04:52,202
Studio downloads its own copy of Python, then installs dbt Core and the DuckDB adapter, all inside its own folder.

59
00:04:52,482 --> 00:04:55,578
Here it took about a minute and a half; the video speeds it up.

60
00:04:56,528 --> 00:05:03,092
Python 3.10.17, and dbt 1.12.6 with one adapter.

61
00:05:03,372 --> 00:05:04,130
Click Finish.

62
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:07,936
This is Studio's home screen, the Workspace Overview.

63
00:05:08,216 --> 00:05:11,442
No projects yet: we create the first one in Module 2.

64
00:05:12,392 --> 00:05:22,318
The status bar at the bottom shows what was installed: dbt 1.12.6, Python 3.10.17, and DuckDB.

65
00:05:23,268 --> 00:05:26,932
At the top right are New Connection, Open Project, and Settings.

66
00:05:27,212 --> 00:05:28,542
That is all we need for now.

67
00:05:29,492 --> 00:05:31,344
To recap: you installed one app.

68
00:05:31,624 --> 00:05:38,009
Studio set up its own Python and dbt Core, with the DuckDB adapter, inside its own folder.

69
00:05:38,289 --> 00:05:41,272
You did not install Python or dbt yourself.

70
00:05:42,222 --> 00:05:47,653
Next, we create the dbt project, and connect it to a repository on GitHub.

71
00:05:55,617 --> 00:05:57,672
A dbt project is a folder.

72
00:05:57,952 --> 00:06:07,908
Models, seeds, tests and macros live in their own subfolders, and one file at the top, dbt_project.yml, gives the project its name and settings.

73
00:06:08,858 --> 00:06:12,802
A second file, profiles.yml, says where dbt runs.

74
00:06:13,082 --> 00:06:17,879
Our profile has one target, dev, and it points at a DuckDB database file.

75
00:06:18,829 --> 00:06:20,933
Before we start, a quick look at Settings.

76
00:06:21,213 --> 00:06:25,570
Under General: the projects directory, where Studio creates new projects.

77
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:36,803
Under dbt Core: the dbt runtime, dbt version one, one point twelve, and the adapters. dbt duckdb is the one for our database.

78
00:06:37,753 --> 00:06:45,139
And Python: Studio runs dbt with its own Python, three point ten, separate from any Python on your computer.

79
00:06:46,089 --> 00:06:47,244
Now, the database.

80
00:06:47,524 --> 00:06:50,939
On the home screen, click New Connection, and choose DuckDB.

81
00:06:55,708 --> 00:06:57,450
Name it nyc taxi dev.

82
00:06:57,730 --> 00:07:03,517
For the file, type a path next to your projects folder: nyc taxi dev dot duckdb.

83
00:07:03,797 --> 00:07:07,576
DuckDB creates the file the first time dbt connects.

84
00:07:07,856 --> 00:07:08,456
Save.

85
00:07:09,406 --> 00:07:10,477
Now the project.

86
00:07:10,757 --> 00:07:15,188
Go back home, open the DBT Project card, and click New Project.

87
00:07:17,761 --> 00:07:19,299
Call it nyc taxi.

88
00:07:19,579 --> 00:07:24,839
Keep the template folders switched on, pick the nyc taxi dev connection, and save.

89
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:29,599
Studio creates the folder and opens it.

90
00:07:29,879 --> 00:07:33,476
Here are the template folders, and the two files we just talked about.

91
00:07:34,426 --> 00:07:44,608
Open dbt project dot yml: the project name is nyc taxi, it uses the profile with the same name, and lists where each kind of file lives.

92
00:07:45,558 --> 00:07:48,292
Let's check that dbt can reach the database.

93
00:07:48,572 --> 00:07:53,227
In the command box under the terminal, type dbt debug, and press Enter.

94
00:07:54,296 --> 00:07:58,761
dbt finds the project and the profile, and connects to DuckDB.

95
00:07:59,041 --> 00:08:00,135
All checks passed.

96
00:08:01,085 --> 00:08:09,530
Before the first commit, add a dot gitignore file at the top of the project, so build output, logs, data and the database stay out of git.

97
00:08:10,783 --> 00:08:14,930
And a short README, so people who open the repository know what it is.

98
00:08:22,176 --> 00:08:24,032
Now a repository on GitHub.

99
00:08:24,312 --> 00:08:32,679
Create a new, empty, public repository called nyc taxi dbt, with no README, gitignore or license.

100
00:08:32,959 --> 00:08:34,511
Here is ours, still empty.

101
00:08:35,461 --> 00:08:37,838
GitHub shows its HTTPS address.

102
00:08:38,118 --> 00:08:38,748
Copy it.

103
00:08:39,698 --> 00:08:44,017
Back in Studio, open the Git tab, and click Initialize Repository.

104
00:08:46,259 --> 00:08:52,550
Stage all the changes, type a message, create the nyc taxi dbt project, and commit.

105
00:08:55,503 --> 00:08:56,683
Click Publish Branch.

106
00:08:56,963 --> 00:09:01,111
Studio asks for the remote: paste the repository address, and add it.

107
00:09:01,391 --> 00:09:03,631
Studio pushes the main branch to GitHub.

108
00:09:04,581 --> 00:09:06,436
Refresh the repository page.

109
00:09:06,716 --> 00:09:12,051
The project is on GitHub: the folders, the two YAML files, the gitignore, and the README.

110
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:22,596
To recap: a DuckDB connection, a dbt project called nyc taxi, a passing dbt debug, and the first commit on GitHub.

111
00:09:22,876 --> 00:09:26,008
From now on, every lesson ends with a commit and a push.

112
00:09:26,288 --> 00:09:27,546
Next, we get the data.

113
00:09:35,483 --> 00:09:40,223
Our raw data is twelve parquet files, one per month of 2025.

114
00:09:40,503 --> 00:09:44,593
They are too big for git, so they stay in a data folder that git ignores.

115
00:09:44,873 --> 00:09:52,502
We load them once into a DuckDB table, raw green trips, and a source, in sources dot yml, tells dbt about it.

116
00:09:53,452 --> 00:09:58,169
The second file is small: a lookup of the 265 taxi zones.

117
00:09:58,449 --> 00:10:01,137
Small CSV files like this one are seeds.

118
00:10:01,417 --> 00:10:05,219
They live in the repository, and dbt loads them as tables.

119
00:10:06,169 --> 00:10:10,351
The files come from the New York City Taxi and Limousine Commission website.

120
00:10:10,631 --> 00:10:16,810
Under 2025, download Green Taxi Trip Records for each month, January to December.

121
00:10:17,090 --> 00:10:18,578
The links are in the description.

122
00:10:19,528 --> 00:10:24,222
Further down the same page, download the Taxi Zone Lookup Table, as CSV.

123
00:10:25,172 --> 00:10:28,248
In Studio, open the nyc taxi project.

124
00:10:28,528 --> 00:10:31,758
Right-click the project, choose New Folder, and call it data.

125
00:10:34,707 --> 00:10:37,064
Inside data, make a folder called raw.

126
00:10:40,512 --> 00:10:46,356
Now move the twelve parquet files into data, raw, and the zone lookup CSV into seeds.

127
00:10:46,636 --> 00:10:48,742
Refresh the explorer, and there they are.

128
00:10:49,692 --> 00:10:51,766
Now load the files into DuckDB.

129
00:10:52,046 --> 00:10:56,235
Open the SQL Editor, and pick the nyc taxi dev connection.

130
00:10:58,399 --> 00:11:00,093
Three statements, one per line.

131
00:11:00,373 --> 00:11:01,861
Create a schema called raw.

132
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:09,256
Create the table raw green trips, from read parquet over all twelve files: use the full path of your data raw folder.

133
00:11:09,536 --> 00:11:10,628
Then count the rows.

134
00:11:11,578 --> 00:11:14,944
Run them one by one, with the green arrow next to each line.

135
00:11:15,224 --> 00:11:20,555
Union by name lines up the columns, even if a month's file has them in a different order.

136
00:11:28,073 --> 00:11:35,718
Five hundred ninety-one thousand, three hundred seventy-five trips in 2025, now in one DuckDB table.

137
00:11:36,668 --> 00:11:39,649
Back in the project, tell dbt about the table.

138
00:11:39,929 --> 00:11:43,456
Right-click models, New File, and call it sources dot yml.

139
00:11:45,813 --> 00:11:49,702
A source called raw, the schema we just made, with a short description.

140
00:11:50,652 --> 00:11:54,535
It has one table, green trips: one row per green taxi trip.

141
00:11:54,815 --> 00:11:55,414
Save.

142
00:11:55,694 --> 00:11:59,365
In a model, source raw, green trips, now points at that table.

143
00:12:00,315 --> 00:12:01,343
Now the Git tab.

144
00:12:01,623 --> 00:12:05,484
Only two files changed: sources dot yml and the CSV.

145
00:12:05,764 --> 00:12:10,278
The parquet files are not here, because the gitignore leaves out the data folder.

146
00:12:11,228 --> 00:12:16,364
Stage all, type a message, declare the raw trips source and add the zone lookup, and commit.

147
00:12:19,918 --> 00:12:21,334
Then click Sync Changes.

148
00:12:21,614 --> 00:12:23,567
Studio pushes the commit to GitHub.

149
00:12:25,018 --> 00:12:30,940
On GitHub, the repository now has the models and seeds folders, and the latest commit message.

150
00:12:31,890 --> 00:12:38,485
To recap: the trip files sit in data raw, outside git, and live in DuckDB as raw green trips.

151
00:12:38,765 --> 00:12:40,150
The zone lookup is a seed.

152
00:12:40,430 --> 00:12:42,819
And sources dot yml declares the source.

153
00:12:43,099 --> 00:12:45,463
Next, we build our first model on top of it.

154
00:12:53,417 --> 00:12:57,402
In the last lesson, we pointed dbt at the raw taxi files.

155
00:12:57,682 --> 00:12:59,845
Now we build on top of them, with a model.

156
00:13:00,125 --> 00:13:05,563
A model is one select statement, saved in one .sql file, in the models folder.

157
00:13:06,513 --> 00:13:12,176
When you run it, dbt builds a view or a table from that select, and names it after the file.

158
00:13:12,456 --> 00:13:15,287
So trips.sql becomes a view called trips.

159
00:13:16,237 --> 00:13:18,337
Here is the project from the last lesson.

160
00:13:18,617 --> 00:13:23,609
Open it, right-click the models folder, choose New File, and call it trips.sql.

161
00:13:26,041 --> 00:13:31,936
Start with a comment that says what one row is: one green taxi trip from 2025.

162
00:13:32,216 --> 00:13:33,065
Then, select.

163
00:13:34,015 --> 00:13:41,428
Pick the columns we need, and give them clear names: the vendor, the pickup and drop-off times, and the pickup and drop-off zones.

164
00:13:42,378 --> 00:13:47,588
Then the passenger count, the distance, how the rider paid, and the money: fare, tip, and total.

165
00:13:48,538 --> 00:13:50,551
Read them from the raw green trips source.

166
00:13:50,831 --> 00:13:55,269
The source function points at the table we loaded and declared in the last lesson.

167
00:13:56,219 --> 00:14:03,318
Finally, keep only trips that started in 2025, and drop the rows with no distance, or no charge.

168
00:14:04,268 --> 00:14:05,512
That is the whole model.

169
00:14:05,792 --> 00:14:07,845
One select, and no create statement.

170
00:14:08,125 --> 00:14:08,742
Save it.

171
00:14:09,692 --> 00:14:11,213
So what happens when you run it?

172
00:14:11,493 --> 00:14:13,677
First, dbt compiles the file.

173
00:14:13,957 --> 00:14:19,821
The Jinja in curly braces becomes plain SQL, and the source call becomes the real table name.

174
00:14:20,771 --> 00:14:24,175
Then dbt wraps your select in a create view statement.

175
00:14:24,455 --> 00:14:26,183
That is the part you never write.

176
00:14:27,133 --> 00:14:32,332
And it sends the statement to DuckDB, which saves the view in the dev database file.

177
00:14:33,282 --> 00:14:34,110
Let's run it.

178
00:14:34,390 --> 00:14:37,429
Above the editor, open the Model menu, and choose Run.

179
00:14:37,709 --> 00:14:39,084
It runs just this model.

180
00:14:40,034 --> 00:14:44,186
The terminal shows one model, trips, created as a view in the main schema.

181
00:14:44,466 --> 00:14:45,818
One pass, no errors.

182
00:14:46,768 --> 00:14:50,463
To look at the rows, open the Model menu again, and choose Preview.

183
00:14:50,743 --> 00:14:54,854
Studio compiles the model, runs its select, and opens the result.

184
00:14:57,035 --> 00:15:03,730
Eleven columns, and five hundred sixty-five thousand trips, out of five hundred ninety-one thousand raw rows.

185
00:15:04,010 --> 00:15:07,357
Scroll right for the distance, the payment type and the amounts.

186
00:15:08,307 --> 00:15:08,948
Close it.

187
00:15:09,228 --> 00:15:15,038
The same rows stay in the Query Results tab, next to the SQL that ran, and a history of your previews.

188
00:15:15,988 --> 00:15:19,061
Want to see exactly what dbt sends to DuckDB?

189
00:15:19,341 --> 00:15:20,658
Model menu, Compile.

190
00:15:23,237 --> 00:15:31,805
The Compiled SQL tab shows your select, with the source call replaced by the real table: nyc taxi dev, raw, green trips.

191
00:15:32,755 --> 00:15:34,692
Close it, and open Run History.

192
00:15:34,971 --> 00:15:41,178
Every dbt command Studio ran is here: dbt run, select trips, one of one passed.

193
00:15:42,128 --> 00:15:43,730
Last step: commit and push.

194
00:15:44,010 --> 00:15:45,100
Open the Git tab.

195
00:15:45,380 --> 00:15:47,967
Trips.sql is listed as a new file.

196
00:15:48,247 --> 00:15:51,790
The target and logs folders are not, because git ignores them.

197
00:15:52,740 --> 00:15:57,809
Stage it with the plus button, type a message, add the trips staging model, and click Commit.

198
00:16:01,435 --> 00:16:04,296
Click Sync Changes, and the commit goes to GitHub.

199
00:16:06,535 --> 00:16:12,009
On GitHub, the models folder now holds trips dot sql, next to sources dot yml.

200
00:16:12,959 --> 00:16:13,692
To recap.

201
00:16:13,972 --> 00:16:21,596
A model is one select in one file. dbt compiles it, wraps it in create view, and runs it in DuckDB.

202
00:16:21,876 --> 00:16:25,133
Preview shows the rows, and the Git tab sends it to GitHub.

203
00:16:25,413 --> 00:16:28,103
Next, we add two more models that build on trips.

204
00:16:36,050 --> 00:16:38,257
Open the nyc taxi project.

205
00:16:38,537 --> 00:16:43,670
First, load the zone lookup into DuckDB: open the Project menu, and choose Seed.

206
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:51,934
dbt reads the CSV, and inserts two hundred sixty-five rows into a table called taxi zone lookup.

207
00:16:52,884 --> 00:16:57,564
To use one model inside another, write ref, and the name of the model or seed.

208
00:16:57,844 --> 00:17:03,017
Not the table name: dbt fills that in, for whichever database you run against.

209
00:17:03,967 --> 00:17:11,729
Each ref is also a dependency. dbt draws the graph from them, and always builds trips before the models that use it.

210
00:17:12,679 --> 00:17:16,029
Right-click models, New File: trips per month dot sql.

211
00:17:19,611 --> 00:17:23,320
Count the trips and the average fare for each month and pickup borough.

212
00:17:28,211 --> 00:17:33,385
From ref trips, joined to ref taxi zone lookup, to get the borough of each pickup zone.

213
00:17:33,665 --> 00:17:34,927
Group by all, and save.

214
00:17:35,877 --> 00:17:38,819
A second model: top pickup zones dot sql.

215
00:17:44,963 --> 00:17:48,568
This time, start with a Jinja variable: set top n to ten.

216
00:17:49,518 --> 00:17:55,046
Then count the trips for each pickup zone, with the zone's name and borough, from the same two refs.

217
00:17:58,251 --> 00:18:01,327
Order by trips, busiest first, and limit to top n.

218
00:18:01,607 --> 00:18:02,206
Save.

219
00:18:03,156 --> 00:18:05,037
Jinja has three kinds of braces.

220
00:18:05,317 --> 00:18:07,295
Double curly braces print a value.

221
00:18:07,575 --> 00:18:10,271
Curly brace and percent run logic, like set.

222
00:18:10,551 --> 00:18:12,518
And curly brace and hash is a comment.

223
00:18:13,468 --> 00:18:17,412
When dbt compiles a model, all of it turns into plain SQL.

224
00:18:17,692 --> 00:18:18,522
Let's see that.

225
00:18:19,472 --> 00:18:23,519
With top pickup zones open, open the Model menu, and choose Compile.

226
00:18:25,711 --> 00:18:28,356
Studio opens the compiled SQL in a window.

227
00:18:28,636 --> 00:18:31,013
The first tab shows what we wrote, with the Jinja.

228
00:18:31,963 --> 00:18:33,387
Switch to Compiled SQL.

229
00:18:33,667 --> 00:18:39,138
Both refs became full table names in the dev database, and limit top n became limit ten.

230
00:18:40,088 --> 00:18:42,225
This is the SQL DuckDB will run.

231
00:18:42,505 --> 00:18:43,457
Close the window.

232
00:18:44,407 --> 00:18:47,143
Now run the whole project: Project menu, Run.

233
00:18:48,093 --> 00:18:52,495
dbt builds trips first, and only then the two models that depend on it.

234
00:18:52,775 --> 00:18:54,261
Three models, no errors.

235
00:18:55,211 --> 00:18:56,445
Now look at the result.

236
00:18:56,725 --> 00:18:59,596
With top pickup zones open, choose Model, Preview.

237
00:19:01,808 --> 00:19:03,552
Ten rows, because of top n.

238
00:19:03,832 --> 00:19:10,608
East Harlem North is the busiest pickup zone of 2025, with a hundred forty-three thousand trips.

239
00:19:11,558 --> 00:19:16,487
Close it, and open Run History: the seed and the run are listed, with their status.

240
00:19:17,437 --> 00:19:18,527
Open the Git tab.

241
00:19:18,807 --> 00:19:20,290
The two new models are listed.

242
00:19:22,269 --> 00:19:27,056
Stage all, type a message, add trips per month and top pickup zones, and commit.

243
00:19:30,559 --> 00:19:32,461
Sync Changes pushes it to GitHub.

244
00:19:35,659 --> 00:19:38,044
To recap: a seed loads a CSV.

245
00:19:38,324 --> 00:19:43,970
Ref names a model and records the dependency, so dbt runs things in the right order.

246
00:19:44,250 --> 00:19:48,419
Jinja adds variables and logic, and compile shows the plain SQL.

247
00:19:48,699 --> 00:19:51,150
Next, we describe our models with properties.

248
00:19:59,117 --> 00:20:02,190
Properties are YAML files that sit next to your SQL.

249
00:20:02,470 --> 00:20:08,533
They describe models, seeds and sources: what a table is, what each column means, and later, the tests.

250
00:20:09,483 --> 00:20:11,869
We already have one: sources dot yml.

251
00:20:12,149 --> 00:20:17,256
Today we add schema dot yml for the models, and seeds dot yml for the zone lookup.

252
00:20:18,206 --> 00:20:19,391
Open the project.

253
00:20:19,671 --> 00:20:22,562
In models, create a new file: schema dot yml.

254
00:20:28,071 --> 00:20:29,614
It is long, so paste it.

255
00:20:29,894 --> 00:20:34,818
One entry per model: a name, a description, and the columns that need explaining.

256
00:20:35,098 --> 00:20:37,051
The code is in the repository.

257
00:20:38,001 --> 00:20:41,090
For example, payment type: one means credit card.

258
00:20:41,370 --> 00:20:44,865
Anyone reading the model learns that here, not from a PDF.

259
00:20:45,145 --> 00:20:45,745
Save.

260
00:20:46,695 --> 00:20:47,557
Now the seed.

261
00:20:47,837 --> 00:20:50,332
In seeds, a new file: seeds dot yml.

262
00:20:54,983 --> 00:21:00,317
This one is short: the zone lookup, where it comes from, and its key column, Location ID.

263
00:21:00,597 --> 00:21:01,196
Save.

264
00:21:04,424 --> 00:21:07,462
Descriptions can also go into the database itself.

265
00:21:07,742 --> 00:21:12,095
Open dbt project dot yml, and add a models block at the end.

266
00:21:13,045 --> 00:21:17,938
Under nyc taxi, persist docs, with relation and columns set to true.

267
00:21:18,218 --> 00:21:18,817
Save.

268
00:21:19,767 --> 00:21:25,086
Run the project from the Project menu, so dbt rebuilds the models with their comments.

269
00:21:26,036 --> 00:21:27,522
Three models, no errors.

270
00:21:29,236 --> 00:21:31,015
Let's look inside DuckDB.

271
00:21:31,295 --> 00:21:39,814
Open the SQL Editor, pick the nyc taxi dev connection, and ask DuckDB for the columns of trips per month, with their comments.

272
00:21:41,488 --> 00:21:42,888
Run it with the green arrow.

273
00:21:45,667 --> 00:21:48,860
Each column carries the description from schema dot yml.

274
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:51,930
Average fare has none, because we didn't describe it.

275
00:21:52,880 --> 00:22:01,598
So one description shows up in three places: in the docs site, which we build next lesson, in the database, and for everyone who reads the code.

276
00:22:02,548 --> 00:22:09,371
Back in the project, open the Git tab: two new YAML files, and the change to dbt project dot yml.

277
00:22:10,321 --> 00:22:14,708
Stage all, type a message, describe the models and the zone seed, and commit.

278
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:21,955
Sync Changes, and it's on GitHub.

279
00:22:25,020 --> 00:22:27,756
To recap: properties are YAML next to your SQL.

280
00:22:28,036 --> 00:22:35,924
Schema dot yml describes the models, seeds dot yml the seed, and persist docs copies the descriptions into DuckDB.

281
00:22:36,204 --> 00:22:38,041
Next, the docs site and the DAG.

282
00:22:45,983 --> 00:22:51,446
Open the project, and create one more model in models: monthly summary dot sql.

283
00:22:56,490 --> 00:23:00,627
First, a block called totals: the trips per month, from ref trips per month.

284
00:23:03,257 --> 00:23:08,478
Then a block called card: the trips paid by credit card each month, straight from ref trips.

285
00:23:09,890 --> 00:23:13,155
Join them by month, and work out the percent paid by card.

286
00:23:13,435 --> 00:23:14,034
Save.

287
00:23:17,298 --> 00:23:22,885
Describe it too: at the end of schema dot yml, paste the entry for monthly summary, and save.

288
00:23:23,835 --> 00:23:25,960
All those refs form a graph: the DAG.

289
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:28,691
Directed, because every arrow points one way.

290
00:23:28,971 --> 00:23:31,676
Acyclic, because no model can depend on itself.

291
00:23:32,626 --> 00:23:39,608
dbt uses it for the build order, to run independent models in parallel, and to show you what a change affects.

292
00:23:40,558 --> 00:23:45,587
Back in monthly summary, open the Model menu, and choose Run plus model, upstream.

293
00:23:45,867 --> 00:23:48,587
That runs this model, and everything it depends on.

294
00:23:49,537 --> 00:23:53,735
Three models, in DAG order: trips, trips per month, then monthly summary.

295
00:23:54,685 --> 00:23:55,623
Now the docs.

296
00:23:55,903 --> 00:24:04,677
Open the Project menu, and choose Generate Docs. dbt reads the project and the database, and writes a small website into target.

297
00:24:06,847 --> 00:24:08,414
Then Project, Serve Docs.

298
00:24:08,694 --> 00:24:11,866
Studio starts a local web server, on port eighty eighty.

299
00:24:14,085 --> 00:24:18,821
In the browser, open localhost, port eighty eighty, and pick monthly summary.

300
00:24:19,101 --> 00:24:24,363
Here is the description from schema dot yml, every column, and what the model depends on.

301
00:24:25,313 --> 00:24:32,133
The button at the bottom right opens the lineage graph for this model: trips, then trips per month, then monthly summary.

302
00:24:32,413 --> 00:24:34,488
The same order dbt just ran.

303
00:24:35,438 --> 00:24:40,282
Back in Studio, the Git tab shows the new model, and the change to schema dot yml.

304
00:24:41,232 --> 00:24:44,932
Stage all, type a message, add the monthly summary, and commit.

305
00:24:49,831 --> 00:24:51,866
Sync Changes, and it's on GitHub.

306
00:24:54,931 --> 00:24:59,650
To recap: refs form the DAG, and dbt uses it for order and impact.

307
00:24:59,930 --> 00:25:03,267
Run plus model builds a model with everything upstream.

308
00:25:03,547 --> 00:25:07,537
And two clicks give you a docs site, with descriptions and the lineage graph.

309
00:25:07,817 --> 00:25:10,631
Next, views or tables: materializations.

310
00:25:18,583 --> 00:25:21,862
dbt can build a model as a view or as a table.

311
00:25:22,142 --> 00:25:27,165
A view stores only the query, so it is always fresh, but every read runs it again.

312
00:25:27,445 --> 00:25:31,663
A table stores the rows: fast to read, and as fresh as the last run.

313
00:25:32,613 --> 00:25:34,902
Right now every model is a view, the default.

314
00:25:35,182 --> 00:25:41,666
We keep trips a view, a thin layer over the files, and make the three marts tables, because people query those.

315
00:25:41,946 --> 00:25:44,428
Incremental and ephemeral models come later.

316
00:25:45,378 --> 00:25:49,158
Open the project, and open dbt project dot yml.

317
00:25:51,121 --> 00:25:54,576
Under nyc taxi, add plus materialized, table.

318
00:25:54,856 --> 00:25:57,168
Every model in the project becomes a table.

319
00:25:57,448 --> 00:25:58,048
Save.

320
00:25:58,998 --> 00:26:00,312
But trips should stay a view.

321
00:26:00,592 --> 00:26:02,228
Open trips dot sql.

322
00:26:02,508 --> 00:26:06,541
At the very top, add a config block: materialized equals view.

323
00:26:06,821 --> 00:26:09,515
A setting in the model wins over the project file.

324
00:26:09,795 --> 00:26:10,395
Save.

325
00:26:11,345 --> 00:26:13,657
Run the project: Project menu, Run.

326
00:26:14,607 --> 00:26:19,884
The log now says sql view model for trips, and sql table model for the other three.

327
00:26:20,834 --> 00:26:22,345
Let's check in DuckDB.

328
00:26:22,625 --> 00:26:28,867
Open the SQL Editor, pick nyc taxi dev, and list the tables in the main schema, with their type.

329
00:26:33,119 --> 00:26:33,690
Run it.

330
00:26:37,298 --> 00:26:38,266
Trips is a view.

331
00:26:38,546 --> 00:26:41,394
The three marts are base tables, and so is the seed.

332
00:26:42,344 --> 00:26:45,824
Back in the project, the Git tab shows the two changed files.

333
00:26:48,078 --> 00:26:52,587
Stage all, type a message, materialize the marts as tables, and commit.

334
00:26:57,249 --> 00:26:59,284
Sync Changes, and it's on GitHub.

335
00:27:02,349 --> 00:27:05,640
To recap: views store the query, tables store the rows.

336
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:12,606
One line in dbt project dot yml sets the default, and a config block in a model overrides it.

337
00:27:12,886 --> 00:27:14,217
Next, we test the data.

338
00:27:22,183 --> 00:27:25,956
A dbt data test is a query that looks for bad rows.

339
00:27:26,236 --> 00:27:28,450
If it returns nothing, the test passes.

340
00:27:28,730 --> 00:27:30,427
If it returns rows, it fails.

341
00:27:31,377 --> 00:27:36,931
dbt has four built in: unique, not null, accepted values, and relationships.

342
00:27:37,211 --> 00:27:43,188
Start with the grain: the column that should be unique and never empty, like the month in monthly summary.

343
00:27:44,138 --> 00:27:46,918
Open the project, and open schema dot yml.

344
00:27:47,198 --> 00:27:49,245
Tests go right under the column they check.

345
00:27:50,812 --> 00:27:55,374
Under borough, in trips per month: accepted values, with the boroughs we expect.

346
00:27:57,445 --> 00:28:02,106
Zone id in top pickup zones is that table's grain: not null and unique.

347
00:28:03,056 --> 00:28:05,192
The same for month in monthly summary.

348
00:28:05,472 --> 00:28:06,072
Save.

349
00:28:07,972 --> 00:28:12,004
And in seeds dot yml, Location ID: not null and unique.

350
00:28:12,284 --> 00:28:12,884
Save.

351
00:28:17,389 --> 00:28:19,456
Run them all: Project menu, Test.

352
00:28:22,307 --> 00:28:25,649
Six pass, one fails: accepted values on borough.

353
00:28:25,929 --> 00:28:28,732
Got one result, so one borough is not in our list.

354
00:28:29,682 --> 00:28:30,495
It is N A.

355
00:28:30,775 --> 00:28:40,589
The zone lookup has a zone two sixty-five, outside of New York City, with the borough N A, and about two hundred fifty trips in 2025 started there.

356
00:28:40,869 --> 00:28:42,963
The data is right; our list was wrong.

357
00:28:43,913 --> 00:28:45,564
Add N A to the list, and save.

358
00:28:48,073 --> 00:28:52,772
To check just this model, open trips per month, and in the Model menu choose Test.

359
00:28:54,330 --> 00:28:56,559
One test on this model, and it passes.

360
00:28:57,530 --> 00:28:59,047
One more command: build.

361
00:28:59,327 --> 00:29:05,773
It seeds, runs and tests, in DAG order, so a failing test stops the models that depend on bad data.

362
00:29:06,723 --> 00:29:07,958
Project menu, Build.

363
00:29:09,027 --> 00:29:11,234
One seed, four models, seven tests.

364
00:29:11,514 --> 00:29:13,127
Twelve passes, no errors.

365
00:29:14,077 --> 00:29:17,760
The Git tab: schema dot yml and seeds dot yml changed.

366
00:29:19,028 --> 00:29:22,288
Stage all, type a message, add data tests, and commit.

367
00:29:26,985 --> 00:29:27,960
Sync Changes.

368
00:29:28,240 --> 00:29:30,338
That's the end of dbt basics.

369
00:29:32,085 --> 00:29:35,031
To recap: tests live in YAML next to the columns.

370
00:29:35,311 --> 00:29:38,122
A failing test told us something true about the data.

371
00:29:38,402 --> 00:29:40,834
And build runs and tests everything, in order.

372
00:29:41,114 --> 00:29:42,612
That's dbt basics.

373
00:29:42,892 --> 00:29:47,141
Next, we ship it: GitHub Actions builds the project on every push.

374
00:29:55,083 --> 00:29:58,645
So far, everything ran on our laptop, in the dev target.

375
00:29:58,925 --> 00:30:02,431
Production should be built somewhere clean, only from what is in git.

376
00:30:03,381 --> 00:30:04,631
That is what CI does.

377
00:30:04,911 --> 00:30:14,431
On every push, GitHub Actions starts a fresh machine, installs dbt, downloads the data, runs dbt build, and publishes the warehouse.

378
00:30:15,381 --> 00:30:16,566
Open the project.

379
00:30:16,846 --> 00:30:22,807
First, requirements dot text in the project root: the same dbt versions Studio uses.

380
00:30:29,678 --> 00:30:38,057
Next, a folder called c i, with its own profiles dot yml: a prod target, and the database path from an environment variable.

381
00:30:43,250 --> 00:30:50,447
Type the profile: nyc taxi, target prod, type duckdb, and the path from env var duckdb path.

382
00:30:51,397 --> 00:30:55,616
The parquet files are not in git, so CI has to get the data itself.

383
00:30:55,896 --> 00:30:58,842
A folder called scripts, and download data dot py.

384
00:31:05,587 --> 00:31:06,536
Paste the script.

385
00:31:06,816 --> 00:31:16,494
It downloads the twelve files into data raw, then loads them into raw green trips in the DuckDB file, the same load we did by hand in the SQL Editor.

386
00:31:16,774 --> 00:31:17,374
Save.

387
00:31:18,324 --> 00:31:19,159
And the workflow.

388
00:31:19,439 --> 00:31:22,175
GitHub looks for it in dot github, workflows.

389
00:31:22,455 --> 00:31:26,125
Make both folders, and a file: dbt build dot yml.

390
00:31:38,874 --> 00:31:40,433
Paste the workflow, and save.

391
00:31:41,383 --> 00:31:45,368
It runs on every push to main, every Monday morning, or by hand.

392
00:31:45,648 --> 00:31:46,891
It may write releases.

393
00:31:47,841 --> 00:32:01,001
The steps: check out the code, set up Python, install dbt, download and load the data, run dbt build with the c i profile, and upload prod dot duckdb to a release called warehouse.

394
00:32:01,951 --> 00:32:04,122
Open the Git tab: four new files.

395
00:32:06,886 --> 00:32:11,649
Stage all, type a message, add CI: build, test and publish the warehouse.

396
00:32:11,929 --> 00:32:12,494
Commit.

397
00:32:15,343 --> 00:32:16,318
Sync Changes.

398
00:32:16,598 --> 00:32:18,201
This push starts the first run.

399
00:32:20,443 --> 00:32:22,543
On GitHub, open the Actions tab.

400
00:32:22,823 --> 00:32:24,345
The run for our commit is there.

401
00:32:25,295 --> 00:32:26,464
Open it, and wait.

402
00:32:26,744 --> 00:32:32,439
A fresh machine installs dbt, downloads and loads the files, and builds the project.

403
00:32:32,719 --> 00:32:34,477
Under a minute later: success.

404
00:32:40,598 --> 00:32:47,136
And under Releases, the warehouse release, with prod dot duckdb attached: the whole warehouse, built by CI.

405
00:32:48,086 --> 00:32:52,810
To recap: a prod profile, pinned versions, a download script, and one workflow.

406
00:32:53,090 --> 00:32:58,818
From now on, every push builds and tests the project on a clean machine, and publishes the warehouse.

407
00:32:59,098 --> 00:33:00,642
Next, we make it a showcase.

408
00:33:08,583 --> 00:33:11,033
Last step: make the project easy to show.

409
00:33:11,313 --> 00:33:16,980
A README that explains it, live docs, a green build badge, and a warehouse anyone can query.

410
00:33:17,930 --> 00:33:21,513
On GitHub, open the repository's Settings, then Pages.

411
00:33:21,793 --> 00:33:26,167
Under Source, choose GitHub Actions, so our workflow can publish the docs.

412
00:33:27,117 --> 00:33:36,334
In Studio, open the workflow file, and paste three more build steps at the end: generate the docs, copy the site files, and upload them for Pages.

413
00:33:37,284 --> 00:33:41,407
And a second job, docs, that runs after build and deploys the site.

414
00:33:41,687 --> 00:33:42,286
Save.

415
00:33:43,236 --> 00:33:44,218
Now the README.

416
00:33:44,498 --> 00:33:54,164
Open it, select all, and paste the new one: a build badge, a docs link, what the project does and where to look, how to query the warehouse, and how to run it yourself.

417
00:33:54,444 --> 00:33:55,044
Save.

418
00:33:55,994 --> 00:34:00,430
Git tab: stage all, type a message, publish docs and README, and commit.

419
00:34:08,826 --> 00:34:09,801
Sync Changes.

420
00:34:10,081 --> 00:34:11,479
The push starts the workflow.

421
00:34:13,926 --> 00:34:18,806
On GitHub, the Actions tab shows the run, now with two jobs: build, then docs.

422
00:34:19,756 --> 00:34:21,424
A minute later, both are green.

423
00:34:22,956 --> 00:34:28,521
The docs are live on GitHub Pages, for anyone, with the lineage graph of the whole project.

424
00:34:29,471 --> 00:34:33,486
And the repository page shows the README, with the green build badge.

425
00:34:34,436 --> 00:34:36,293
And anyone can query the warehouse.

426
00:34:36,573 --> 00:34:43,384
With DuckDB, attach prod dot duckdb straight from the GitHub release, read only, and select from the marts.

427
00:34:44,334 --> 00:34:51,153
East Harlem North leads, with a hundred forty-three thousand trips: built by CI, and read over the internet.

428
00:34:52,103 --> 00:34:53,280
That's Project 1.

429
00:34:53,560 --> 00:35:04,556
Raw files and a seed, models with ref, descriptions, docs and the DAG, tables where they matter, tests, CI on every push, live docs, and a warehouse anyone can query.

430
00:35:04,836 --> 00:35:08,356
All built in Rosetta DBT Studio, and all on GitHub.
